{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1+log(3)/log(2); the full solution set is {1, log_2(6)}.","reason":"With u=2^x, the equation becomes (u-2)(u-6)=0. Thus x=1 or x=log_2(6)=1+log(3)/log(2), so D is a solution.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1969_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h043"],"id":"quadratic-exponential-substitution","label":"Substitute for the repeated power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-exponential-substitution":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224","cnm_h048","cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224","cnm_h048","cnm_h045","cnm_h044","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1 or 5","reason":"Let t = 3^x > 0. Then t^2-10t+9 = (t-1)(t-9) = 0, so x = 0 or 2. Consequently x^2+1 is 1 or 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Explicit substitution and logarithmic notation are valid methods, not universally necessary steps; the exponential expression can also be factored directly."],"questionId":"1972_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitute-and-factor","label":"Factor the substituted quadratic","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-and-factor":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224","cnm_029"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224","cnm_029"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitute-and-complete-square","label":"Complete the substituted square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-and-complete-square":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_221","cnm_029"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_221","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2","reason":"With t=3^x>0, the equation becomes 9t²-28t+3=(9t-1)(t-3)=0. Both roots are positive and give distinct real values x=-2 and x=1, so there are two solutions.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1982_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042"],"id":"positive-exponential-substitution","label":"Turn the equation into a quadratic","methodDirectNodeIds":{"positive-exponential-substitution":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 7/2","reason":"Let A = 2ˣ - 4 and B = 4ˣ - 2. Since A³ + B³ - (A + B)³ = -3AB(A + B), one factor must vanish. These give x = 2, x = 1/2, or, with t = 2ˣ > 0, t² + t - 6 = 0 and hence x = 1. Their sum is 7/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1991_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h042","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_148","cnm_220"],"id":"cube-identity-and-exponential-substitution","label":"Use the cube-of-a-sum structure","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-identity-and-exponential-substitution":["cnm_h049","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_224","math:quadratic-exponential-substitution"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h049","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_224","math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_h042","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_148","cnm_220"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","status":"mapped","total":4}
